          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is GymProLuxe legit or a scam?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is a real business. Its domains lead to one long-running store, the UK company register lists an active company behind its app, and the product has been reviewed independently and stocked by UK retailers. The caution is not about legitimacy but about terms: its refund policy, product pages and warranty page describe returns and replacements differently, so confirm the terms for your order in writing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where is GymProLuxe based?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In the United Kingdom. Its terms are governed by UK law, the company linked to its app has a registered office in London, and its shipping policy says international orders, including US orders, are sent from its UK warehouse. That affects delivery times and means a US return is an international parcel, so ask where returns go and who pays postage before ordering.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is GymProLuxe sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We could not confirm a current GymProLuxe listing on Amazon.com on 16 October 2026; our searches found none and Amazon search pages did not load for us that day. A UK newspaper article mentioned the set on Amazon, possibly the UK site. The brand sells directly through its UK store and a US storefront, and through several UK retailers. If you want a similar band-and-bar system on Amazon, the Gorilla Bow Original carded on this page is the closest we found.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does GymProLuxe have a lifetime warranty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, on its bands, with conditions. The warranty page covers manufacturing defects and quality issues from normal use, not misuse or modification. Claims are made by emailing a photo. The replacement part is free, but the brand charges shipping on it unless the claim is made within 30 days of purchase and the defect is confirmed. Product pages describe the cover more broadly than that.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you return a GymProLuxe band and bar set?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The formal refund policy allows a return request within 30 days of receipt for unused items in original packaging, may charge a restocking fee for change of mind, and says sale items cannot be returned. Product pages promise a no-questions-asked 30-day money-back guarantee, and one bundle page mentions 100 days. Ask support which applies to your order before you buy.
            </p>
